\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description\
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 An NSMethodSignature records type information for the arguments and return value of a method. It\'27s used to forward messages that the receiving object doesn\'27t respond to\'d0most notably in the case of distributed objects. An NSMethodSignature is typically created using NSObject\'27s \b methodSignatureForSelector:\b0  instance method. It\'27s then used to create an NSInvocation, which is passed as the argument to a \b forwardInvocation:\b0  message to send the invocation on to whatever other object can handle the message. In the default case, NSObject invokes \b doesNotRecognizeSelector:\b0 , which raises an exception. For distributed objects, the NSInvocation is encoded using the information in the NSMethodSignature and sent to the real object represented by the receiver of the message.\
\fs14\
\fs28 An NSMethodSignature presents its argument types by index with the \b getArgumentTypeAtIndex:\b0  method. The hidden arguments for every method, \b self\b0  and \b _cmd\b0 , are at indices 0 and 1 respectively. The arguments normally specified in a message invocation follow these. In addition to the argument types, an NSMethodSignature offers the total number of arguments with \b numberOfArguments\b0 , the total stack frame length occupied by all arguments with \b frameLength\b0  (this varies with hardware architecture), and the length and type of the return value with \b methodReturnLength\b0  and \b methodReturnType\b0 . Finally, applications using distributed objects can determine if the method is asynchronous with the \b isOneway\b0  method.\
\fs14\
\fs28 For more information about the nature of a method, including the hidden arguments, see \i Object-Oriented Programming and the Objective-C Language\i0 .\
\fs14\
